and C_{Ag} = [CN^{-}] + [HCN] + \sum_{1}^{n} nK_{sn}^{O}[CN^{-}]^{n-1}  [23]Since  C_{Ag}  is small, all activity coefficients are assumed to be unity, and the only mononuclear complex that need be considered is  Ag(CN)_{2}^{-} . Equating [22] and [23] yields [Ag^{+}]^{2} = K_{s0}^{O}(AgCN)\{1 + K_{s2}^{O} + [H^{+}][CN^{-}]/K_{a}^{O}\}  [24]Elimination of  [Ag^{+}]  in this equation is accomplished with [H^{+}] - K_{w}/ [H^{+}] = -[H^{+}][CN^{-}]/K_{a}^{O}  [25]which yields the polynomial [H^{+}]^{5} + [H^{+}]^{4}K_{a}^{O}\{1 + K_{s2}^{O} - K_{s0}^{O}(AgCN)/K_{a}^{O2}\}- 2[H^{+}]^{3}K_{w} - 2[H^{+}]^{2}K_{a}^{O}K_{w}(1 + K_{s2})  + [H^{+}]K_{w}^{2} + K_{a}^{O}K_{w}^{2}(1 + K_{s2}) = 0  [26]In eqs [25] and [26],  K_{w}  is the ionization product of water (1 x  10^{-14}  at 298 K). Solving eq [26] by the Newton-Raphson iteration method, it is found that  [H^{+}] = 9.6 \times 10^{-8} mol dm^{-3} ,  [Ag^{+}] = 2.1 \times 10^{-6} (eq [24]) , and from eq [22],  C_{Ag} = 4.1 \times 10^{-6} mol dm^{-3} . The identical result is obtained if hydrolysis is completely neglected, and  C_{Ag} = 2\{K_{s0}^{O}(AgAg(CN)_{2})\}^{1/2} .

